          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher-order+logic"} higher-order
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+type+theory"} intuitionistic type theory
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔏
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{L}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝔏
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(\mathfrak{L})
            out of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax"} syntax
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔏
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{L}
            %strong free topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔏
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(\mathfrak{L}_0)
            is the result of this construction when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔏
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{L}_0
            is ‘pure’ type theory i.e. the only types are 1 (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+type"}> unit type
            ),
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+type"}> natural numbers type
            ), and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+of+propositions"}> type of propositions
            ) lacking relations beyond the bare necessities.
          %p
            As
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔏
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{L}_0
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial object
            in the appropriate category of type theories, the free topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔏
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(\mathfrak{L}_0)
            is itself initial in the corresponding category of toposes (with a natural numbers object) and suitable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+morphisms"} logical morphisms
            and is, therefore, also known as the
            %strong initial topos
            = succeed "." do
              %sup#fnref:1
                %a{:href => "#fn:1", :rel => "footnote"} 1
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+language"} internal language
            of the free topos is precisely pure (intuitionistic) higher order type theory. In 1978,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jim+Lambek"} Jim Lambek
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Phil+Scott"} Phil Scott
            exploited this connection in order to prove properties of intuitionistic type theory by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof+theory"} proof-theoretic
            means. It was observed by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Freyd"} Peter Freyd
            then that the concept of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freyd+cover"} Freyd cover
            permits to give conceptual proofs of their findings. The following lemma and proposition is a replication of his ideas.
          .num_lemma
            %h6#lemma Lemma
            %p
              For any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              with a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{1}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freyd+cover"} Freyd cover
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi C
                      %mo ^
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat{C}
              is
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small-projective"} small-projective
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo =
                    %mover
                      %mi C
                      %mo ^
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mover
                      %mi C
                      %mo ^
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma = \widehat{C}(1, -) \colon \widehat{C} \to Set
              preserves any colimits that exist.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              To check that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Γ
                      %mi op
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mover
                        %mi C
                        %mo ^
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma^{op} \colon \widehat{C}^{op} \to Set^{op}
              preserves limits, it suffices to check that the composite
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mover
                        %mi C
                        %mo ^
                      %mi op
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Γ
                          %mi op
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mi op
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mn 2
                          %mo −
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat{C}^{op} \stackrel{\Gamma^{op}}{\to} Set^{op} \stackrel{2^-}{\to} Set
            %p
              preserves limits, because the contravariant power set functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mo −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P = 2^-
              is monadic. But it is easily checked that this composite is the contravariant representable given by
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 2
                      %mo →
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (2, \mathbf{1}, 2 \to \Gamma(\mathbf{1}))
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
              in the free topos
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+object"} connected
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+object"} projective
              in the sense that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo =
                    %mi hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma = \hom(1, -) \colon \mathcal{T} \to Set
              preserves finite
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p We divide the argument into three segments:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  The
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-functor"} hom-functor
                  preserves
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+limits"} finite limits
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Artin+gluing"} Artin gluing
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freyd+cover"} Freyd cover
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi 𝒯
                          %mo ^
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat{\mathcal{T}}
                  is also a topos. Observe that
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒯
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
                  is equivalent to the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+category"} slice
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi 𝒯
                          %mo ^
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi M
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat{\mathcal{T}}/M
                  where
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi M
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                  is the object
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi ∅
                          %mo ,
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mi ∅
                          %mo →
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\emptyset, \mathbf{1}, \emptyset \to \Gamma(\mathbf{1}))
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi π
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mover
                          %mi 𝒯
                          %mo ^
                        %mo →
                        %mi 𝒯
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi \colon \widehat{\mathcal{T}} \to \mathcal{T}
                %p
                  Since
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒯
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
                  is initial,
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi π
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
                  is a retraction for the unique logical functor
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mi 𝒯
                          %mo →
                          %mover
                            %mi 𝒯
                            %mo ^
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \colon \mathcal{T} \to \widehat{\mathcal{T}}
              %li
                %p
                  We have maps
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒯
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %mover
                          %mi 𝒯
                          %mo ^
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi i
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mi i
                        %mo −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ≅
                        %mover
                          %mi 𝒯
                          %mo ^
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mi i
                        %mo −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}(1, -) \to \widehat{\mathcal{T}}(i 1, i-) \cong \widehat{\mathcal{T}}(1, i-)
                  (the isomorphism comes from
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ≅
                          %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i 1 \cong 1
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
                  is logical), and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi 𝒯
                          %mo ^
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mi i
                        %mo −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %mi 𝒯
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi π
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mi π
                        %mi i
                        %mo −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ≅
                        %mi 𝒯
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat{\mathcal{T}}(1, i-) \to \mathcal{T}(\pi 1, \pi i-) \cong \mathcal{T}(1, -)
                  since
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi π
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
                  is logical and retracts
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝒯
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}(1, -)
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %p
              Finally, since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}(1, -)
              is a retract of a functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi 𝒯
                      %mo ^
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi i
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat{\mathcal{T}}(1, i-)
              that preserves finite colimits (by the lemma, and the fact that the logical functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              preserves finite colimits), it must also preserve finite colimits.
          %p
            This is important because it implies that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            of the free topos satisfies the following properties:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                = succeed ":" do
                  %em disjunction property
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/or"} or
                Q” is provable in the empty
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
                = succeed ".)" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            %li
              %p
                The
                = succeed ":" do
                  %em existence property
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in A
                such that
                = succeed "\u201D" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(x)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+element"} global element
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mn 1
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\colon 1\to A
                such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(x)
                is provable in the empty context. (Again, this is clearly a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructivity
                property.)
            %li
              %p
                The
                = succeed ":" do
                  %em negation property
            %li
              %p
                All numerals in the free topos are “standard”, i.e., the global sections functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo =
                      %mi hom
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi 𝒯
                      %mo →
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma = \hom(1, -): \mathcal{T} \to Set
                preserves the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
                (because
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
                can be characterized in terms of finite colimits and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
          %h3#foundations_2 Foundations
          %p
            In the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations+of+mathematics"} foundations of mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jim+Lambek"} Jim Lambek
            proposed to use the free topos as ambient world to do mathematics in; see (
            %a{:href => "#LambekWorld"}> Lambek 2004
            ). Being syntactically constructed, but universally determined, with higher-order
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+type+theory"} intuitionistic type theory
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+language"} internal language
            he saw it as a reconciliation of the three classical schools of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/philosophy+of+mathematics"} philosophy of mathematics
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formalism"} formalism
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/platonism"} platonism
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionism"} intuitionism
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#LS11"} Lambek-Scott 2011
          %h3#remark_two_properties_of_the_free_topos Remark: Two properties of the free topos
          %p
            Lambek and Scott mention in their 1986 monograph (
            %a{:href => "#LambekScott86"}> pp.viii, 233, 250
            ) two further remarkable properties of the free topos
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                All maps
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                      %mo →
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R\to R
                in the free topos represent continuous functions. Note that this is not the same as to say that Brouwer’s theorem (“all functions
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                      %mo →
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R\to R
                are continuous”) is true in the free topos, which is not the case.
            %li
              %p
                The natural numbers object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
                is an (external)
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+object"} projective object
          %p
            The first result is attributed to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Joyal"} André Joyal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Makkai"} Michael Makkai
            and a manuscript by Friedman and Scedrov, presumably (
            %a{:href => "#FriedmanScedrov83"}> 1983
            ).
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freyd+cover"} Freyd cover
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Artin+gluing"} Artin gluing
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntactic+category"} syntactic category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+modality"} adjoint modality
          %h2#links Links
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/264652/brouwers-theorem-in-the-free-topos"} MO Brouwer’s theorem in the free topos?
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "https://nforum.ncatlab.org/discussion/6665/free-topos/#Item_0"} nForum discussion
          %h2#references References
          %p The free topos was first constructed by H. Volger. A second construction appears in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jim+Lambek"}> Jim Lambek
              ,
              %em From Types to Sets
              , JPAA
              %strong 36
              (1980) pp.113-164.
          %p Freyd’s proof of the above properties appears first in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jim+Lambek"}> Jim Lambek
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Phil+Scott"} Phil Scott
              %em Intuitionist Type Theory and the Free Topos
              , JPAA
              %strong 19
              (1980) pp. 215-257.
          %p On the relation between the proof and the topos-theoretic techniques in the proof see
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A. Scedrov,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Phil+Scott"} Phil Scott
                %em A note on the Friedman Slash and Freyd Covers
                , pp. 443-452 in
                %em The LEJ Brouwer Centenary Symposium
                , North Holland Amsterdam 1982.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jim+Lambek"}> Jim Lambek
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Phil+Scott"} Phil Scott
                %em New proofs of some intuitionistic principles
                , Zeit. für Math. Logik
                %strong 29
                (1983) pp. 493-504.
          %p For textbook accounts of the free topos see
          %ul
            %li#LambekScott86
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jim+Lambek"}> Jim Lambek
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Phil+Scott"} Phil Scott
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Introduction+to+Higher-Order+Categorical+Logic"} Introduction to Higher-Order Categorical Logic
                , Cambridge UP 1986. (
                %a{:href => "https://synrc.com/publications/cat/Category%20Theory/Categorical%20Logic/Lambek%20J.,%20Scott%20P.J.%20Introduction%20to%20Higher%20Order%20Categorical%20Logic.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Freyd"}> P. Freyd
                , A. Scedrov,
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categories%2C+Allegories"} Categories, Allegories
                , North-Holland Amsterdam 1990. (1.(10)31, p.192)
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Elephant"} Elephant
                F3, to appear.
          %p For dependent choice in intuitionistic type theory:
          %ul
            %li#FriedmanScedrov83
              H. M. Friedman, A. Scedrov,
              %em Set existence property for intuitionistic theories with dependent choice
              , APAL
              %strong 25
              no.2 (1983) pp.129-140.
          %p
            For J. Lambek’s views on the role of the free topos in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations+of+mathematics"} foundations of mathematics
            see:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                J. Couture,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jim+Lambek"} Jim Lambek
                %em Philosophical Reflections on the Foundations of Mathematics
                , Erkenntnis
                %strong 34
                (1991) pp.187-209.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jim+Lambek"}> Jim Lambek
                ,
                %em Are the traditional philosophies of mathematics really incompatible?
                , Math. Intelligencer
                %strong 16
              %p (1994) pp.56–62.
            %li#LambekWorld
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jim+Lambek"}> Jim Lambek
                ,
                %em What is the world of mathematics?
                , APAL
                %strong 126
                (2004) pp.149-158. (
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.mcgill.ca/barr/lambek/pdffiles/WorldMath.pdf"}> draft
                )
            %li#LS11
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jim+Lambek"}> Jim Lambek
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Phil+Scott"} Phil Scott
                %em Reflections on Categorical Foundations of Mathematics
                , pp.171-185 in Sommaruga (ed.),
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Foundational Theories of Classical and Constructive Mathematics
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.site.uottawa.ca/~phil/papers/LS11.final.pdf"} draft
          .footnotes
            %hr/
            %ol
              %li#fn:1
                %p
                  This is not to be confounded with the gadget of the same name in
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SGA4"} SGA4
                  (1972, p.313) i.e. the topos
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi sh
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi ∅
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sh(\emptyset)
                  of sheaves on the empty topological space aka the one point category, also called the
                  %em empty topos
                  there. In this context the appropriate maps are
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphisms"} geometric morphisms
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:1", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
        .revisedby
